<chapter
 id="lecture06">
#title("Functional List-Processing (2)")

#para('\

Sometimes, a list-processing function is partial in the sense that it
is not well-defined for all of the lists. For instance, the function
#dyncode("list_head") for returning the head element of a given list
is defined only if the given list is non-empty. As in the previous chapter,
the list is a linear list that is borrowed (the symbol #keycode("!")):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
exception ListSubscriptExn of ()
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_head(xs: !List0_vt(a)): a
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_head(xs) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_cons(x, _) => x
| _(* list_vt_nil() *) => $raise ListSubscriptExn()
)
//
')

Note that the code #dyncode("$raise ListSubscriptExn()") is for
raising an exception (in the case where #dyncode("xs") is empty),
which is to be explained in details later. An exception is a resource,
too: it is linear, and a handler is to consume it. A function that raises an
exception while it holds a linear value (a list for instance) abandons the
value, which is a leak; for this reason (and also because the type system does
not permit a linear value to be used inside a try-expression unless the value is
local to it) I prefer to turn a partial function into a total one that returns
options when linear values are involved. The type #stacode("Option_vt(T)")
is for linear options (it is a datavtype with the constructors
#dyncode("Some_vt") and #dyncode("None_vt")), and
#dyncode("list_head_opt") is a total function corresponding to
#dyncode("list_head"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_head_opt
  (xs: !List0_vt(a)): Option_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_head_opt(xs) =
(
case+ xs of list_vt_cons(x, _) => Some_vt(x) | _ => None_vt()
)
//
')

A linear option is to be consumed, too: the function
#dyncode("option_vt_free") frees an option of non-linear contents, and a
pattern like #dyncode("~Some_vt(x)") consumes the option and binds its content to
#dyncode("x"). The function returning the last element
of a given list is also partial as it is only defined
for a non-empty list. The following function
#dyncode("list_last_opt") returns an option to indicate
whether the last element of a given list is found:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_last_opt
  (xs: !List0_vt(a)): Option_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_last_opt(xs) = let
//
fun
loop
(x0: a, xs: !List0_vt(a)): a =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => x0
| list_vt_cons(x1, xs) => loop(x1, xs)
)
//
in
//
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => None_vt()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => Some_vt(loop(x, xs))
//
end // end of [list_last_opt]
//
')

Note that the inner function #dyncode("loop") in the
body of #dyncode("list_last_opt") is tail-recursive.

')

#para('\

Before moving on, I would like to point out a very common mistake in
(functional) list-processing. First and foremost, a list is not meant
to be used like an array. The following (partial) function
#dyncode("list_get_at") does the so-called list-subscripting:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_get_at
(xs: !List0_vt(a), n: int): a
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_get_at
  (xs, n) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  $raise ListSubscriptExn()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  if n <= 0 then x else list_get_at<a>(xs, n-1)
)
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1,10,100)") and the index
#dyncode("1"), #dyncode("list_get_at") returns the element
#dyncode("10").  Another common name for #dyncode("list_get_at") is
#dyncode("list_nth"). Clearly, the time-complexity of
#dyncode("list_get_at") is O(n) (while array-subscripting is
O(1)-time). It is almost always a poor programming style to process
the elements in a list by calling #dyncode("list_get_at") (as the
resulting code is likely to be prohibitively inefficient
time-wise). For the purpose of illustration, let us take a look at the
following two functions for tallying the integers contained in a given
list:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
list_tally1
  (xs: !List0_vt(int)): int = let
  var fopr = lam@(res: int, x: int): int => res + x
in
  list_foldleft<int><int>(xs, 0, fopr)
end // end of [list_tally1]
//
fun
list_tally2
  (xs: !List0_vt(int)): int = let
//
fun
loop
(xs: !List0_vt(int), i: int, n: int, res: int): int =
  if i < n then loop(xs, i+1, n, res + list_get_at<int>(xs, i)) else res
//
in
  loop(xs, 0, list_length<int>(xs), 0)
end // end of [list_tally2]
//
')

Given a list #dyncode("xs") of length n, the function
#dyncode("list_tally1") is O(n)-time while the function
#dyncode("list_tally2") is O(n#sup("2"))-time. (In
#dyncode("list_tally2"), the list #dyncode("xs") is passed as an argument to
#dyncode("loop") instead of being captured by a closure passed to
#dyncode("int_foldleft"): a closure cannot capture a borrowed linear value.)
List-processing should
be done in the efficient style of #dyncode("list_tally1") rather than
in the terribly inefficient style of #dyncode("list_tally2"). In
general, please try to avoid doing list-subscripting repeatedly if the
involved index cannot be bounded by a small constant!

')

#para('\

Let us see more functions for performing functional
list-processing in the following presentation and an
example of functional programming at the end that illustrates some
typical use of list-processing functions.

')

#para('\

A commonly used (higher-order) function is often referred to as
list-map, which takes a list and a function and returns a newly
constructed list consisting of all of the elements obtained from
applying the given function to each element in the given list:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
{b:t@ype}
list_map
(xs: !List0_vt(a), fopr: &(a) -<clo1> b): List0_vt(b)
//
implement
{a}{b}
list_map
(
  xs, fopr
) = auxlst(xs, fopr) where
{
//
fun
auxlst
(xs: !List0_vt(a), fopr: &(a) -<clo1> b): List0_vt(b) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => list_vt_cons(fopr(x), auxlst(xs, fopr))
)
//
} (* end of [list_map] *)
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)") and the
integer square function, #dyncode("list_map") returns the list
consisting of #dyncode("1"), #dyncode("4"), #dyncode("9"),
#dyncode("16"), and #dyncode("25"). The given list is borrowed and the result is a
new list, which has to be freed by the caller:

#dats2xhtml('\
val sq =
let
  var fopr = lam@(x: int): int => x * x
in
  list_map<int><int>(xs5, fopr)
end
val () = println! ("squares = ", sq)
val () = list_vt_free<int>(sq)
')

')

#para('\

With #dyncode("list_map"), we can build #dyncode("list_cross")
as follows for computing the cross product of two given lists. The
elements of the result are flat tuples (the type #stacode("@(a, b)")),
which are not allocated on heap:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a,b:t@ype}
list_cross
(xs: !List0_vt(a), ys: !List0_vt(b)): List0_vt(@(a, b))
//
implement
{a,b}(*tmp*)
list_cross
  (xs, ys) = let
//
typedef ab = @(a, b)
//
in
//
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => let
    var fopr = lam@(y: b): ab => @(x, y)
    val res1 = list_map<b><ab>(ys, fopr)
    val res2 = list_cross<a,b>(xs, ys)
  in
    list_vt_append<ab>(res1, res2)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
//
end // end of [list_cross]
//
')

For instance, given two lists #dyncode("(0, 1)")
and #dyncode("(2, 3, 4)"), #dyncode("list_cross") returns a newly
constructed list consisting of the following six pairs: #dyncode("(0,
2)"), #dyncode("(0, 3)"), #dyncode("(0, 4)"), #dyncode("(1, 2)"),
#dyncode("(1, 3)"), and #dyncode("(1, 4)"). (In the version of the GC era,
the code was a call to #dyncode("list_map") nested in the closure passed to
another call to #dyncode("list_map"), and a call to #dyncode("list_concat");
here the closure would have to capture the borrowed list #dyncode("ys"),
which is not possible, and so the first list is traversed directly.)

')

#para('\

A function rather similar to #dyncode("list_map") is
#dyncode("list_foreach"), which takes a list and a procedure (i.e., a
function returning void) and applies the procedure to each element in
the list (so as to create some effects):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_foreach
(xs: !List0_vt(a), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void): void
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_foreach
(
  xs, fwork
) = loop(xs, fwork) where
{
//
fun
loop
(xs: !List0_vt(a), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void): void =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => ()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => (fwork(x); loop(xs, fwork))
)
//
} (* end of [list_foreach] *)
//
')

')

#para('\

Another commonly used (higher-order) function is often referred to as
list-filter, which takes a list and a predicate (i.e., a function
returning a boolean value) and returns a newly constructed list
consisting of all of the elements in the given list that satisfy the
given predicate:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_filter
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_filter
(
  xs, test
) = auxlst(xs, test) where
{
//
fun
auxlst
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): List0_vt(a) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  if test(x)
    then list_vt_cons(x, auxlst(xs, test)) else auxlst(xs, test)
  // end of [if]
)
//
} (* end of [list_filter] *)
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)")
and the predicate for testing whether an integer is even,
#dyncode("list_filter") returns the list consisting of #dyncode("2")
and #dyncode("4").

')

#para('\

Given a list #dyncode("xs"), the following function
#dyncode("list_remdup") removes all of the elements in #dyncode("xs")
that have already appeared previously:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_remdup
(xs: !List0_vt(a), eqfn: (a, a) -> bool): List0_vt(a)
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_remdup(xs, eqfn) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() =>
  list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs) => let
    var test = lam@(x: a): bool => ~eqfn(x0, x)
    val ys = list_filter<a>(xs, test)
    val zs = list_remdup<a>(ys, eqfn)
    val () = list_vt_free<a>(ys)
  in
    list_vt_cons(x0, zs)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
)
//
')

The implementation of #dyncode("list_remdup") should clearly remind
one of the sieve of Eratosthenes, which is to be given a stream-based
implementation later. Note that the function argument
#dyncode("eqfn") is an envless function (the type
#stacode("(a, a) -&gt; bool")) rather than a flat closure passed by reference: it is
captured by the closure #dyncode("test"), which is possible for
an envless function (a value) but not for a flat closure passed by
reference. The intermediate list #dyncode("ys") is freed as soon as it is no
longer needed.

')

#para('\

The list-processing function #dyncode("list_map") processes every
element in its list argument and so does #dyncode("list_filter").
Sometimes, we need a list-processing function that stops immediately
after certain condition is met. For instance, we may want to locate
the index of the first element in a given list that satisfies some test,
which can be done by calling the following function #dyncode("list_find_index"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_find_index
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): int
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_find_index
  (xs, test) = let
//
fun
loop
(xs: !List0_vt(a), i: int, test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): int =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => ~1
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) =>
  if test(x) then i else loop(xs, i+1, test)
)
//
in
  loop(xs, 0, test)
end // end of [list_find_index]
//
')

For instance, given the list #dyncode("(1, 2, 3)") and the predicate
for testing whether an integer is even, #dyncode("list_find_index")
returns #dyncode("1") (which is the index for the element #dyncode("2")
in the given list). Note that #dyncode("~1") (negative 1) is returned
if no element satisfying the given predicate is found.

')

#para('\

Given a list #dyncode("xs") and a predicate #dyncode("test"),
#dyncode("list_exists") returns true if and only if there exists one
element in #dyncode("xs") satisfing #dyncode("test"), and
#dyncode("list_forall") returns true if and only if every element in
#dyncode("xs") satisfies #dyncode("test"). The former can be
readily implemented based on a direct call to
#dyncode("list_find_index"); the latter is implemented by a direct recursion
(a call to #dyncode("list_find_index") on the negation of #dyncode("test") would
require a closure that captures the closure #dyncode("test")):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_exists
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): bool
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_forall
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(a) -<clo1> bool): bool
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_exists(xs, test) =
list_find_index<a>(xs, test) >= 0
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_forall(xs, test) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => true
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => test(x) && list_forall<a>(xs, test)
)
//
')

')

#para('\

Given a list (x#sub("0"), ..., x#sub("n-1")) of length n, its indexed
version refers to the list of pairs in which the elements are of the form
(i, x#sub("i")) for i ranging from 0 to n-1.  For a function that
processes a given list, there is often a meaningful variant of the
function that processes the indexed version of the list. For instance,
the following function #dyncode("list_imap") is such a variant of
#dyncode("list_map"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
{b:t@ype}
list_imap
(xs: !List0_vt(a), fopr: &(int, a) -<clo1> b): List0_vt(b)
//
implement
{a}{b}
list_imap
(
  xs, fopr
) = auxlst(0, xs, fopr) where
{
//
fun
auxlst
(i: int, xs: !List0_vt(a), fopr: &(int, a) -<clo1> b): List0_vt(b) =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => list_vt_cons(fopr(i, x), auxlst(i+1, xs, fopr))
)
//
} (* end of [list_imap] *)
//
')

Let us use #dyncode("list_iexists") and #dyncode("list_iforall") to
refer to the variants of #dyncode("list_exists") and
#dyncode("list_forall"), respectively, for processing indexed lists.
The code implementing #dyncode("list_iforall") is as follows
(and #dyncode("list_iexists") is omitted for brevity):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t@ype}
list_iforall
(xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(int, a) -<clo1> bool): bool
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
list_iforall(xs, test) = loop(0, xs, test) where
{
//
fun
loop
(i: int, xs: !List0_vt(a), test: &(int, a) -<clo1> bool): bool =
(
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => true
| list_vt_cons(x, xs) => test(i, x) && loop(i+1, xs, test)
)
//
} (* end of [list_iforall] *)
//
')

')

#para('\

I have so far presented a variety of functions for processing lists in a
functional style. I would like to conclude the chapter with an example
of functional programming that can concretely demonstrate some typical use of
list-processing functions in practice.

')

#para('\

The famous 8-queen puzzle asks the player to find ways to put eight
queen pieces on a chess board such that no queen piece can attack any
other ones. In other words, no two queen pieces can be put on the same
row, the same column, or the same diagnal. This puzzle can be readily
solved with a tree-based search. Intuitively, a node represents a partial
solution where there
are #emphasis("n") queen pieces (for some #emphasis("n") less than or
equal to 8) on the first #emphasis("n") rows of the chess board such
that no one piece can attack any other pieces. Given a node, another
node extending it with one more queen piece is considered its
child. A node is represented as a list of integers; the length of the list
refers to the number of queen pieces on the chess board; the integers
(between 0 and N-1, inclusive) in the list refer to the reversely listed
column positions of the queen pieces. As a node is a linear list, a node is
linear, too, and each node that is made is to be consumed or freed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
\#define N 8
//
vtypedef node = List0_vt(int)
//
')

The initial node (representing the empty partial solution), the length of a node
and the freeing of a node (and of a list of nodes) are as follows:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
node_init(): node = list_vt_nil()
//
fun
node_length(nx: !node): int = list_length<int>(nx)
//
fun
node_free(nx: node): void = list_vt_free<int>(nx)
//
fun
nodes_free(nxs: List0_vt(node)): void =
(
case+ nxs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => ()
| ~list_vt_cons(nx, nxs) => (node_free(nx); nodes_free(nxs))
)
//
')

The function #dyncode("test_safety") checks whether the column position
of the queen that is put at the head of a node is safe for the other queens:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
test_safety
(
xs: !List0_vt(int)
) : bool = let
//
in
//
case+ xs of
| list_vt_nil() => true
| list_vt_cons(x0, xs) => let
    var test = // abs: absolute value
    lam@(i: int, x: int): bool => (x0 != x && abs(x0-x) != (i+1))
  in
    list_iforall<int>(xs, test)
  end // end of [list_vt_cons]
//
end // end of [test_safety]
//
')

The function #dyncode("node_get_children") is supposed to be called to obtain
all of the child nodes of a given node. A candidate child is constructed
with a copy of the given node (since the given node is only borrowed), and a
candidate that is not safe is freed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
node_get_children
  (nx: !node): List0_vt(node) = let
//
fun
loop(nx: !node, x: int): List0_vt(node) =
if
(x >= N)
then list_vt_nil()
else let
  val nx1 = list_vt_cons(x, list_vt_copy<int>(nx))
in
  if test_safety(nx1)
    then list_vt_cons(nx1, loop(nx, x+1))
    else (node_free(nx1); loop(nx, x+1))
end // end of [else]
//
in
  loop(nx, 0)
end // end of [node_get_children]
//
')

')

#para('\

With
#dyncode("node_get_children"),
we can readily implement #dyncode("node_dfsenum") as follows
for enumerating in the depth-first manner all of the nodes contained
in the tree rooted at a given node. The given node is consumed (it becomes the head
of the result) and so are the children, each of which is passed to a recursive
call:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
node_dfsenum
(nx0: node): List0_vt(node) = let
  val nxs = node_get_children(nx0)
in
  list_vt_cons(nx0, auxlst(nxs))
end // end of [node_dfsenum]
//
and
auxlst
(nxs: List0_vt(node)): List0_vt(node) =
(
case+ nxs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(nx, nxs) =>
  list_vt_append<node>(node_dfsenum(nx), auxlst(nxs))
)
//
')

In order to find all of the solutions to the 8-queen puzzle,
we just need to keep all of the nodes of length 8 that are contained
in the tree rooted at the initial node (representing the empty partial
soloution). The nodes that are not kept are freed:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
keep_solutions
(nxs: List0_vt(node)): List0_vt(node) =
(
case+ nxs of
| ~list_vt_nil() => list_vt_nil()
| ~list_vt_cons(nx, nxs) =>
  if node_length(nx) = N
    then list_vt_cons(nx, keep_solutions(nxs))
    else (node_free(nx); keep_solutions(nxs))
)
//
fun
QueenSolve(): List0_vt(node) =
keep_solutions(node_dfsenum(node_init()))
//
')

A call to #dyncode("QueenSolve") returns a list of 92 solutions, which the
caller is to free (by calling #dyncode("nodes_free")).

')

#para('\

The presented code for solving 8-queen puzzle is of the kind of
high-level functional programming style I intend to advocate
throughout the book. It should soon be clear that we can reap even
more benefits from programming in this way when (linear) lazy streams
are used in place of functional lists.

')

#para('\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/06/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter (the files #filename("listfun2.hats"),
#filename("queens.hats") and #filename("queens_dfs.hats") and a test in #filename("lecture06.dats"), which was
compiled with #command("patscc") and run with the address sanitizer, no
leak is reported). \

')#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture06"-->

